 place three years  after the loss of civilization this was  now hundreds of years so to sort of  explore a different facet of that I like  the idea of going out into the  wilderness and doing something very  authentically and as realistically as  possible so really going out you know  far distances and trying to  things and experience time and make make  the environments very immersive there  was just so many aspects to the show  that I that I loved how much did you and  Steven discuss the sort of over like  what's going on on the rest of the  planet coming up with like a show Bible  or a series Bible that can be followed  and how much is it sort of like let's  design the first season then we can see  what happens well Steve had a general  pitch for because we went in when we  sold the show we went in with two  episodes and I had a visual presentation  of my take on it and he had a pitch for  the rest of season one and then he had a  broader Pitchford sort of following  season so he had a pretty good idea of  where the show is gonna go very early on  well I'm curious like have you guys  thought about what happened to like  Detroit or what happened to this place  or you know what I mean yeah I mean you  know I don't want to get into spoilers  but you know we you know there's a lot  of traveling in this show so we end up  in different places and so you'll see  you know possibly later the season and  in other seasons ideally some of you  know what has happened in different  places that we find familiar one of the  things I thought was so cool in the  first episode is the community in what  basically how you can felt that felt  very real yeah this would really be what  happened if everyone lost their sight  with the ropes and everything can you  sort of talk about the challenges of  building all of that from the ground up  yeah I mean I think it's it's a  challenge to build that idea from the  ground up but it's also the fun of this  kind of world building it's one of the  things that I love about filmmaking and  so what we did very early on was we  actually had a think tank so we had a  gentleman that was blind a couple that  was blind and a survivalist and an  evolutionary biologist and sort of  everybody was just kind of brainstorming  and spitballing ideas that could play  into the narrative and also the  world-building  and then we put together a team so for  actors they had a blindness coach with  Jost rachet who's who's here with us  today and we had a movement coach that  was sort of I'd say primarily in charge  of the sort of cultural aspects of the  show in term  like okay you're a mountain tribe what's  that going to be like you know what are  the games you play the songs you sing  and you know all of that to sort of  create a rich tapestry for all the  different groups and tribes you know in  this world and so some of that was sort  of outside of blindness and some of that  was incorporated with the with the  blindness aspect one of the things again  no spoilers but there are some pretty  vicious fight scenes yeah  in the show and what's really cool about  it is that these characters are blind  yes having these intricate fight scenes  what the hell does it like to put those  things together and design them because  you're you I mean there's nothing like  it like I've seen in this kind of scale  yeah I mean it's tricky again fun tricky  but tricky and so you know if you look  at the big there's a big battle scene in  episode 1 and that we started  brainstorming very very early on in the  prep process and so one of the first  things we did was we wanted to just kind  of come up with fight style ideas and  ideas for you know how the different  factions would fight so if you're the  mountain tribe and you're gonna be up on  a wall what are the kinds of things that  you can do if you're coming from below  and going up the hill what are the  things you could do  what's the armor like what are the  weapons like you know getting into sort  of Judo style fighting because it means  there's contact and the idea of once you  make contact you don't want to lose  contact right until you are victorious  in some way and so we started to spit  ball all these ideas and gathering up  you know sort of a toolset for it and  then I would talk to the stunt team  about the kind of emotional beats and a  narrative that I want to take place  within the scene and then we kind of  built it all out from there but it was  always everything was always kind of  driven from this idea of blindness did  you you did the first three episodes yes  the series is eight yes was there ever  in your mind and do I want to helm all  eight of these well I thought that I was  gonna do all of them when I signed on to  do the show and then when I saw the  first set of outlines of the whole  season I thought just the scope of the  show there was no way that I would be  able to do all eight it would be do to  show a disservice just because the show  was so big and  so in terms of the prep and honestly I  had prepped it was sort of on the scale  of a large movie and I was you know  served barely prepared for my three just  because there's a lot to it because a  big cast route in really distant  locations we had this sort of piece a  lot of things together there's big  action there's a lot of animals there's  just you know a lot of elements to deal  with and so there's just no way I could  have done the other the other five what  did you learn making the show you know  there's a lot of talk you might have a  second season it's been picked up I  don't know the exact specifics but what  did you learn making the show that you  think will be able to be applied to the  second season because the first season  is always like building the  infrastructure and figuring it out well  I think a lot in terms of behavior and  sort of rules just because we we did a  lot of work in prep so that by the time  we were in the first day of shooting  people were pretty well versed in the  language of being blunt in terms of  blocking what do you you know what to do  with your eyes you know how to sort of  guide somebody to your hand if you need  to guide somebody to your hand the you  know to using the hand sign language you  know there was just a lot of that work  that went in very early but you know  then you shoot for nine months and so  people are like living and immersed in  that language and they get better and  better and better at it and new things  are you know people come up with new  things every day and so I just think  having that knowledge going into a  second season it would be fantastic I  gotta go thank you so much for your time  thank you Congrats on the series thank  you I'll talk to you later

